  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA low-pressure system that is currently centered over the \nIowa/Missouri/Illinois tri-state area will amble into the Middle \nOhio Valley this weekend. Scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to traverse the area today, then precipitation coverage \nshould become more widespread as the system steers a cold front \ntowards the area tonight and Sunday.\n\nPrecipitable water values are expected to rise into the\nneighborhood of 2 inches as southerly flow funnels moisture- \nrich air up from the Gulf in advance of the front. This should \nfacilitate periods of heavy downpours, which could lead to some \nlocalized flash flooding in poor drainage areas or with training\nstorms on Sunday.\n\nModel soundings continue to display potential for isolated strong to \nsevere thunderstorms Sunday afternoon, though this potential appears \nto be contingent on whether there is enough of a gap in activity or \ncloud cover for the environment to destabilize. Damaging winds would \nbe the primary concern for any severe storms that do develop, though \na tornado isn't completely out of the question either. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front crosses the area Sunday night, though \ndaily chances of showers and thunderstorms are expected to linger as \nan upper trough and low remain in the vicinity through the beginning \nof the work week. The trough is then projected to recede north as \nhigh pressure builds into the southeastern US from the Atlantic mid \nto late week.",
